= a
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Direct <matrix multiplication> gives $MN=\begin{pmatrix}5&18\\7&25\end{pmatrix}$. The three <determinants> are $\det M=8-9=-1$, $\det N=4-3=1$, and $\det(MN)=125-126=-1$. Hence $\boxed{\det(MN)=(-1)(1)=\det M\det N}$, as required.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The two-by-two <matrix inverse> formula gives
$$
M^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}-4&3\\3&-2\end{pmatrix},\quad N^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}4&-3\\-1&1\end{pmatrix},\quad(MN)^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}-25&18\\7&-5\end{pmatrix}.
$$
Multiplication in the reversed order gives
$$
\boxed{N^{-1}M^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}-25&18\\7&-5\end{pmatrix}=(MN)^{-1}.}
$$
The order reversal is needed because applying the inverse undoes the original two <linear maps> in reverse sequence.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Take $A=B=I_2$, which are <invertible matrices>. Then $\det(A+B)=\det(2I_2)=4$, whereas $\det A+\det B=2$. This proves that the <determinant is not additive>. Being a <multilinear map> of separate columns does not imply additivity in the entire <matrix>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Again take $A=B=I_2$. Their sum is also invertible, but $(A+B)^{-1}=I_2/2$, while $A^{-1}+B^{-1}=2I_2$. Thus <matrix inversion is not additive>, <even> when every inverse in the proposed identity exists.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use $A=M$ and $B=N$ from part (a). The proposed unreversed product is
$$
A^{-1}B^{-1}=\begin{pmatrix}-19&15\\14&-11\end{pmatrix},
$$
which differs from the actual inverse $\begin{pmatrix}-25&18\\7&-5\end{pmatrix}$. Both original <matrices> are <invertible matrices>, so this is a valid counterexample. The universally valid order is $\boxed{(AB)^{-1}=B^{-1}A^{-1}}$.

= c
{parent=5}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The product of a $3\times n$ <matrix> with another $3\times n$ <matrix> requires its inner dimensions $n$ and three to match. Thus $\boxed{n=3}$ is necessary and sufficient for $M^2$ to be defined.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <transpose> has shape $n\times3$. Multiplying two copies requires $3=n$, so $\boxed{n=3}$.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The shapes in $MM^T$ are $(3\times n)(n\times3)$, whose inner dimensions always match. Hence **every <positive integer> $n$ is allowed**, and the resulting <matrix> is $3\times3$.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

<Matrix addition> requires equal shapes. The shapes $3\times n$ and $n\times3$ agree exactly when $\boxed{n=3}$.

= v
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=v}

A two-sided <matrix inverse> requires a <square matrix>, so $\boxed{n=3}$ is necessary. It exists only if that <square matrix> is also an <invertible matrix>, equivalently $\det M\ne0$; choosing the dimension alone does not ensure invertibility.

= 6
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=6}

For a nonzero separated solution $U=X(x)T(t)$, <separation of variables> gives $X''/X=T'/T=-\lambda$. The zero <Dirichlet boundary conditions> <force> $X=\sin nx$, $\lambda=n^2$, with <integers> $n\geq1$; the time factors are $e^{-n^2t}$. Expanding the initial profile in this <Fourier sine series> requires only the two already displayed spatial modes. Thus
$$
\boxed{U(x,t)=3e^{-t}\sin x-e^{-9t}\sin3x.}
$$
Both terms individually solve the <heat equation>, vanish at the endpoints and give the required initial profile at $t=0$. The initial function is also $4\sin^3x\geq0$ on the bar; the later solution stays nonnegative and tends to zero as <heat> leaves through the fixed-temperature ends.

= 7
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= i
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Parametrize the line by $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t,t)$, $0\leq t\leq1$. Its <tangent vector> is $(1,1,1)$. On this line the three <force> components sum to $\mu t+(1-\lambda)t+(\lambda-1-\mu)t=0$. Therefore the <line integral> is $\boxed{\int\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=0}$ for every parameter choice.

= ii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

On the specified <curve> $\mathbf r(t)=(t,t,t^2)$, the <tangent vector> is $(1,1,2t)$ and
$$
\mathbf F(\mathbf r(t))=(\mu t^2,\ t-\lambda t^2,\ t^2+(\lambda-2-\mu)t).
$$
Their <dot product> is $t+(\lambda-\mu-4)t^2+2t^3$. Hence
$$
\boxed{\int\mathbf F\cdot d\mathbf r=\int_0^1[t+(\lambda-\mu-4)t^2+2t^3]dt=\frac{\lambda-\mu-1}{3}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The field is smooth on the <simply connected> domain $\mathbb R^3$. Its <curl> is $\nabla\times\mathbf F=(2\lambda-2,2\mu,0)$, so a necessary and sufficient condition for a <conservative vector field> is
$$
\boxed{\lambda=1,\qquad\mu=0.}
$$
At those values it is explicitly the <gradient> of $\Phi=xy-x^2/2-yz+z^2/2$. Equality of the two previous path <integrals> would impose only $\lambda-\mu=1$, which is weaker: <coinciding path integrals need not imply a conservative field>. For example $\lambda=2,\mu=1$ makes those two <integrals> equal but leaves nonzero curl.

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Multiply the <Taylor series> $\cos x=1-x^2/2+O(x^4)$ by $(1-x)^{-1}=1+x+x^2+O(x^3)$. Counting the constant as the first nonzero term gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{\cos x}{1-x}=1+x+\frac{x^2}{2}+O(x^3).}
$$

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The identity $\sin^3x=(3\sin x-\sin3x)/4$ makes the lower-order cancellation explicit. Expand the two sines through degree seven:
$$
\boxed{\sin^3x=x^3-\frac{x^5}{2}+\frac{13x^7}{120}+O(x^9).}
$$
These are the first three nonzero terms; there is no linear term.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

For $F(x)=e^{e^x}$, the <chain rule> gives $F'=e^xe^{e^x}$ and $F''=(e^x+e^{2x})e^{e^x}$. Thus $F(0)=e$, $F'(0)=e$ and $F''(0)=2e$, yielding
$$
\boxed{e^{e^x}=e+ex+ex^2+O(x^3).}
$$

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $1/f(x)=b_0+b_1x+b_2x^2+O(x^3)$ and multiply by $a_0+a_1x+a_2x^2+O(x^3)$. The product equals one, so its coefficients give $a_0b_0=1$, $a_0b_1+a_1b_0=0$ and $a_0b_2+a_1b_1+a_2b_0=0$. Since $a_0=f(0)\ne0$,
$$
\boxed{\frac1{f(x)}=\frac1{a_0}-\frac{a_1}{a_0^2}x+\frac{a_1^2-a_0a_2}{a_0^3}x^2+O(x^3).}
$$
This is the initial part of the general <reciprocal of a power series> recurrence.

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <exponential function> definitions of the <hyperbolic functions> and <Euler's formula> give
$$
\sinh(ix)=\frac{e^{ix}-e^{-ix}}2=i\sin x,\qquad\cosh(ix)=\frac{e^{ix}+e^{-ix}}2=\cos x.
$$
Dividing these identities wherever $\cos x\ne0$ gives $\boxed{\tanh(ix)=i\tan x}$. The two undivided identities hold for every <complex number> $x$; the quotient identity excludes its poles.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Adding and subtracting the <exponential function> <power series> cancels the appropriate <odd> or <even> terms:
$$
\boxed{\sinh x=\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{x^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}=x+\frac{x^3}{3!}+\cdots,\qquad\cosh x=\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{x^{2n}}{(2n)!}=1+\frac{x^2}{2!}+\cdots.}
$$
Both series converge absolutely for every <complex number> $x$, as follows from the <ratio test>.

= c
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Two applications of <integration by parts> give $I=\cosh x\sin x-\int\sinh x\sin x\,dx$ and $\int\sinh x\sin x\,dx=-\sinh x\cos x+I$. Thus an <antiderivative> is $[\cosh x\sin x+\sinh x\cos x]/2$. Evaluating its endpoints gives
$$
\boxed{\int_0^{\pi/2}\cosh x\cos x\,dx=\frac12\cosh\frac\pi2.}
$$

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
