= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $n\geq6$ and $n-1<x<n$, monotonicity gives $(x+1)^{-2}<n^{-2}<x^{-2}$. Integrating over each unit interval and summing proves the <integral test> tail bounds
$$
\boxed{\int_5^\infty\frac{dx}{(x+1)^2}=\frac16<\sum_{n=6}^\infty\frac1{n^2}<\int_5^\infty\frac{dx}{x^2}=\frac15.}
$$
The inequalities remain strict because the very first rectangle already has a strictly positive difference from either bounding <integral>.

\Image[paper-1-reciprocal-square-rectangles.png]
{title=Unit rectangles between two shifted reciprocal-square curves}
{description=Original comparison for the first five intervals of the infinite tail. Each rectangle has height $1/n^2$ on $[n-1,n]$, above $(x+1)^{-2}$ and below $x^{-2}$.}

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Add the supplied five-term <partial sum> to each side of the tail inequality. Since $1/6=600/3600$ and $1/5=720/3600$,
$$
\boxed{\frac{5869}{3600}<\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac1{n^2}<\frac{5989}{3600}.}
$$
Numerically this is approximately $1.63028<\sum n^{-2}<1.66361$.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Apply the <integral bounds for a finite harmonic sum> to the omitted terms from thirteen through one hundred:
$$
\ln\frac{101}{13}<\sum_{n=13}^{100}\frac1n<\ln\frac{100}{12}.
$$
Adding the given $H_{12}=86021/27720$ gives $5.15338\ldots<H_{100}<5.22348\ldots$. Both bounds lie between $5.15$ and $5.25$, so every value they permit rounds to the same two <significant figures>:
$$
\boxed{H_{100}=\sum_{n=1}^{100}\frac1n\simeq5.2\quad\text{to two significant figures}.}
$$
The rounding follows from the bounds, without computing all the intervening terms.

